[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router2
diagnosis_text:
- Affected component: The BGP routing subsystem (FRRouting/bgpd) on router2 is down.
- Mechanism: From pc1, pings to 200.1.1.2 return “Destination Net Unreachable” sourced by 195.11.14.1 (router1), which happens when router1 lacks a route to the 200.1.1.0/24 network. On router1, BGP shows the neighbor 193.10.11.2 stuck in “Active” (not Established), so no route to 200.1.1.0/24 is learned. Meanwhile, the inter-router link itself is healthy: both router1:eth0 and router2:eth0 report “Link detected: yes,” and their /24 interconnects are up, disproving a physical link failure.
- Localization: On router2, the FRR service is failed and bgpd is not running (“frr.service … Active: failed (failed)” and “bgpd is not running”). Router1’s FRR is running, but has no BGP session with router2.
- Root cause: The FRRouting service (bgpd) on router2 is not running, causing the eBGP session to be down and routes to 200.1.1.0/24 to be absent on router1.
- Corrective fix: Restart FRR on router2. Command: systemctl restart frr (or service frr restart). After bgpd comes up, the eBGP session to 193.10.11.1 should establish, router1 will learn 200.1.1.0/24, and connectivity from pc1 to pc2 will be restored. Also verify FRR is enabled to start on boot (systemctl enable frr) and check /etc/frr/frr.conf for correctness and logs for the cause of the crash/stop.

=== END ===